WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:05.810
For the past 9 minutes the Space Shuttle has
been powering its way through the atmosphere.

00:00:05.810 --> 00:00:11.130
Propelled forward by 2 massive solid rocket
boosters, completing their duties 2 minutes

00:00:11.130 --> 00:00:15.179
into the flight, they are now falling back
to earth where they will be refurbished and

00:00:15.179 --> 00:00:16.369
reused.

00:00:16.369 --> 00:00:22.100
The final push comes from the orbiter's 3
main engines, being fed liquid oxygen and

00:00:22.100 --> 00:00:27.900
liquid hydrogen from the colossal orange external
tank at an astonishing rate.

00:00:27.900 --> 00:00:33.489
Having drained the 700 tonnes of fuel and
oxidiser from its tanks the time has come

00:00:33.489 --> 00:00:38.750
to shut down the powerful main engines and
jettison the external tank.

00:00:38.750 --> 00:00:43.110
The astronauts inside the orbiter will find
themselves transitioning from a exilerating

00:00:43.110 --> 00:00:48.970
thundering journey to a serene silent cruise,
becoming weightless the moment the engines

00:00:48.970 --> 00:00:50.360
power down.

00:00:50.360 --> 00:00:53.860
The light of the shining blue earth below
them flood through the windows of the crew

00:00:53.860 --> 00:00:54.860
cabin.

00:00:54.860 --> 00:00:59.250
The journey to orbit has ended, but their
mission has just begun.

00:00:59.250 --> 00:01:04.220
The space shuttle was not just a rocket, it
was a mobile laboratory, a home in space,

00:01:04.220 --> 00:01:09.130
and it came with all the tools the astronauts
needed to not just survive , but to complete

00:01:09.130 --> 00:01:11.580
complicated tasks in space.

00:01:11.580 --> 00:01:17.450
A sanctuary amidst the unforgiving expanse
of space, the orbiter provided a secure haven,

00:01:17.450 --> 00:01:22.830
sustaining a breathable atmosphere, facilitating
precise maneuvers, a payload bay capable of

00:01:22.830 --> 00:01:27.750
carrying massive spacecraft like the Hubble
Space Telescope, and the machinery needed

00:01:27.750 --> 00:01:30.000
to repair them when needed.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:39.759
This is the insane engineering of the Space
Shuttle Orbiter

00:01:39.759 --> 00:01:43.310
The astronauts' jobs begin the moment they
reach orbit.

00:01:43.310 --> 00:01:47.720
The orbiter has released the external tank
and it’s beginning its journey back through

00:01:47.720 --> 00:01:50.719
the atmosphere where it will disintegrate.

00:01:50.719 --> 00:01:55.899
This is the last view anyone will have of
the external tank intact, and it was vital

00:01:55.899 --> 00:02:01.110
that photos were taken of it to check for
any issues that could have occurred on ascent.

00:02:01.110 --> 00:02:06.010
To do this the commander of the orbiter needs
to pitch the shuttle onto its back using its

00:02:06.010 --> 00:02:07.880
maneuvering system.

00:02:07.880 --> 00:02:12.990
Scattered all over the orbiter are smaller
rocket nozzles, part of the reaction control

00:02:12.990 --> 00:02:14.099
system.

00:02:14.099 --> 00:02:19.120
This removable module in the nose of the aircraft
was the forward reaction control system.

00:02:19.120 --> 00:02:25.300
[Footage]Inside there are two spherical tanks,
an oxidiser tank filled with nitrogen tetroxide

00:02:25.300 --> 00:02:28.610
and a fuel tank filled with monomethyl hydrazine.

00:02:28.610 --> 00:02:31.360
This fuel oxidiser combination is unique.

00:02:31.360 --> 00:02:33.600
It doesn’t need an ignition source.

00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:35.070
It’s hypergolic.

00:02:35.070 --> 00:02:38.849
Meaning they spontaneously ignite upon contact
with each other.

00:02:38.849 --> 00:02:42.560
But, both substances were extremely toxic.

00:02:42.560 --> 00:02:45.830
Producing ominous orange clouds of toxic gas.

00:02:45.830 --> 00:02:50.130
Breathing this in would cause your lungs to
rapidly fill with fluid, and once in your

00:02:50.130 --> 00:02:54.330
bloodstream it interferes with the blood's
ability to carry oxygen.

00:02:54.330 --> 00:02:58.440
Anyone working with the propellants had to
wear pressurized breathing suits to prevent

00:02:58.440 --> 00:03:00.409
any chance of contact.

00:03:00.409 --> 00:03:05.549
The astronauts also had exclusion zones around
these nozzles on space walks to prevent any

00:03:05.549 --> 00:03:07.980
danger of the chemicals coming back aboard.

00:03:07.980 --> 00:03:13.640
It’s an undesirable fuel to work with in
many ways, but its advantages were why it

00:03:13.640 --> 00:03:14.880
was used.

00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:20.330
These hypergolic fuels were extremely reliable,
igniting on contact with each other removed

00:03:20.330 --> 00:03:23.360
any need for complicated ignition systems
that could fail.

00:03:23.360 --> 00:03:28.080
[REF] They could be stored for longer periods
than cryogenic fuels that would have to be

00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:33.140
continually vented as temperatures rose, which
makes cryogenic fuels unsuitable for longer

00:03:33.140 --> 00:03:38.159
missions like the space shuttle, with the
longest space shuttle mission lasting 18 days

00:03:38.159 --> 00:03:39.380
this was critical.

00:03:39.380 --> 00:03:40.380
[FOOTAGE]

00:03:40.380 --> 00:03:45.069
The propellants could also be quickly mixed
in precise volumes to provide precise and

00:03:45.069 --> 00:03:47.409
instantaneous thrust control.

00:03:47.409 --> 00:03:51.959
This was important when maneuvering in close
proximity to another spacecraft.

00:03:51.959 --> 00:03:56.560
The propellants are fed into the combustion
chamber through pressurization of the tanks

00:03:56.560 --> 00:03:58.410
with high pressure helium.

00:03:58.410 --> 00:04:04.250
Held in spherical kevlar composite tanks with
titanium inner liners, an extremely lightweight

00:04:04.250 --> 00:04:09.590
tank, just 10 kilograms in weight, but nearly
a half meter in diameter.

00:04:09.590 --> 00:04:13.709
Spherical tanks are the most weight efficient
form of pressure vessel, with the highest

00:04:13.709 --> 00:04:15.670
volume to weight ratio.

00:04:15.670 --> 00:04:21.720
Ideally all rockets could use spherical tanks,
but ultimately diameters increase too much

00:04:21.720 --> 00:04:26.910
as volume requirements rise and it’s preferable
to minimize the cross-sectional area of the

00:04:26.910 --> 00:04:29.100
rocket to reduce drag.

00:04:29.100 --> 00:04:33.780
There is no turbopump needed here; the back
pressure from the high pressure helium provides

00:04:33.780 --> 00:04:38.000
enough flow rate for the purposes of these
low power rocket motors.

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:43.020
In the forward reaction control system pod
there are 12 main thrusters and there are

00:04:43.020 --> 00:04:46.210
12 in each of the left and right rear pods
too.

00:04:46.210 --> 00:04:53.970
For a total of 38 main thrusters, each capable
of providing 3870 newtons of thrust with a

00:04:53.970 --> 00:04:56.970
specific impulse of 289 seconds.

00:04:56.970 --> 00:05:01.580
Sometimes even finer control was needed for
delicate docking procedures

00:05:01.580 --> 00:05:07.500
and for this the orbiter had 6 smaller vernier
thrusters capable of producing as little as

00:05:07.500 --> 00:05:08.500
111 newtons.

00:05:08.500 --> 00:05:14.910
The same amount of force you would need to
lift an 11 kilogram weight here on earth.

00:05:14.910 --> 00:05:19.850
With a total of 44 reaction control thrusters
scattered around the orbiter the astronauts

00:05:19.850 --> 00:05:21.930
needed a way to control them.

00:05:21.930 --> 00:05:26.750
The controls were located at the commander's
seat on the left side of the cockpit and here

00:05:26.750 --> 00:05:29.280
on the left side of the aft flight deck.

00:05:29.280 --> 00:05:34.240
The rear position was added to allow the commander
to look out this rear window while maneuvering

00:05:34.240 --> 00:05:38.770
for rendezvous with other spacecraft or releasing
payloads.

00:05:38.770 --> 00:05:43.660
Each station had a square-knobbed controller
used for translational movements, in and out,

00:05:43.660 --> 00:05:45.830
side to side and up and down.

00:05:45.830 --> 00:05:50.690
And a more traditional flight stick, which
was used to control attitude, pitch, roll

00:05:50.690 --> 00:05:51.690
and yaw.

00:05:51.690 --> 00:05:52.690
[[REF]

00:05:52.690 --> 00:05:56.580
There was also a flight computer where desired
attitude and velocity changes could be entered

00:05:56.580 --> 00:05:57.889
with a keypad.

00:05:57.889 --> 00:06:03.240
Of course this was all fly by wire the commanders
inputs were all fed into a computer which

00:06:03.240 --> 00:06:06.740
interpreted the commands and decided the best
course of action.

00:06:06.740 --> 00:06:10.110
There was only one notable exception to this
fly by wire system.

00:06:10.110 --> 00:06:11.110
]

00:06:11.110 --> 00:06:16.150
I mean, there is nothing mechanically linked
to any of the control surfaces or anything.

00:06:16.150 --> 00:06:20.091
Everything goes through the computer, everything
you touch goes through the computer and the

00:06:20.091 --> 00:06:21.220
computer generates everything.

00:06:21.220 --> 00:06:25.590
We are concerned about things called single
event upset, and you've probably heard of

00:06:25.590 --> 00:06:30.410
this before, where a cosmic ray comes in and
upsets the computers and you can recover from

00:06:30.410 --> 00:06:31.410
anything.

00:06:31.410 --> 00:06:36.080
But because there was no way to raise the
landing gear, we were concerned a single event

00:06:36.080 --> 00:06:43.120
upset if there was an automatic computer way
to lower the wheels that you could get your

00:06:43.120 --> 00:06:46.310
wheels lowered on orbit and not be able to
come home.

00:06:46.310 --> 00:06:49.470
So we always made that mechanical.

00:06:49.470 --> 00:06:54.800
And so once we got down to under 300 feet,
200 feet, that was the pilot's main job was

00:06:54.800 --> 00:06:56.750
to lower the wheels manually.

00:06:56.750 --> 00:07:00.620
Putting them back in place required ground
crew to push them back in.

00:07:00.620 --> 00:07:06.080
So, because ionizing radiation in space could
potentially trigger the computers to lower

00:07:06.080 --> 00:07:11.289
the landing gear, and with no way of raising
them back up, they were lowered manually.

00:07:11.289 --> 00:07:14.860
The landing gears were held in place by a
hooked lock that was released

00:07:14.860 --> 00:07:19.960
after the pilot flipped the protective covers
of these two buttons, an arming button and

00:07:19.960 --> 00:07:22.669
button labeled “dn” for down.

00:07:22.669 --> 00:07:27.750
When this happened a hydraulic actuator released
the hook holding the landing gears in place

00:07:27.750 --> 00:07:29.610
and allowed them to lower.

00:07:29.610 --> 00:07:31.889
The first day of the mission is now starting.

00:07:31.889 --> 00:07:34.139
[REF] The astronauts begin their tasks.

00:07:34.139 --> 00:07:38.370
The pilot and commander seated in the left
and right seats in front of the instrument

00:07:38.370 --> 00:07:43.039
panel are busy working towards the first rendezvous
with intelsat.

00:07:43.039 --> 00:07:47.970
Through the forward windows the astronauts
can glimpse a distant bright dot, the satellite

00:07:47.970 --> 00:07:50.319
that they are maneuvering towards.

00:07:50.319 --> 00:07:54.710
These weren’t the only windows on board,
there were additional overhead windows, along

00:07:54.710 --> 00:07:59.590
with rear windows looking into the payload
bay, which for now remains closed.

00:07:59.590 --> 00:08:03.000
These windows were key to the reusability
of the shuttle.

00:08:03.000 --> 00:08:07.970
The windows had to be durable enough to survive
space debris impacts and capable of resisting

00:08:07.970 --> 00:08:09.840
the temperatures of re-entry

00:08:09.840 --> 00:08:14.349
The Space Shuttle's window structure had multiple
layers of glass, each tailored to withstand

00:08:14.349 --> 00:08:15.919
different conditions.

00:08:15.919 --> 00:08:20.840
The pressure pane was engineered to endure
the cabin pressure in space; it was made from

00:08:20.840 --> 00:08:26.909
15.7 millimeters thick aluminosilicate glass
that was tempered to be mechanically strong.

00:08:26.909 --> 00:08:31.569
On the outer edge of this pane, a red film
was added to reflect back the heat into the

00:08:31.569 --> 00:08:35.919
vehicle to not be lost to space, while still
letting visible light through.

00:08:35.919 --> 00:08:42.279
The outermost pane, or the thermal pane, was
also 15.7 millimeters thick and capable of

00:08:42.279 --> 00:08:46.649
resisting temperatures up to 482 degrees celsius.

00:08:46.649 --> 00:08:51.750
There was also a large space between these
two panes, which minimized heat transfer between

00:08:51.750 --> 00:08:54.620
the outer shell and the inner crew cabin.

00:08:54.620 --> 00:08:59.709
On one discovery mission one of these windows
was struck by space debris, leaving a small

00:08:59.709 --> 00:09:02.660
crater in the window, but it did not break.

00:09:02.660 --> 00:09:03.660
[Footage]

00:09:03.660 --> 00:09:09.060
If it had, the astronauts inside were in no
danger, as there was a center redundant pane,

00:09:09.060 --> 00:09:12.540
serving as a failsafe for both the thermal
and pressure panes.

00:09:12.540 --> 00:09:18.690
It was 33 millimeters thick and made out of
fused silica glass the same material that

00:09:18.690 --> 00:09:21.829
laboratory crucibles and beakers are made
of.

00:09:21.829 --> 00:09:26.680
The rear windows looking into the payload
area did not have the outer thermal pane since

00:09:26.680 --> 00:09:31.510
they would not be exposed to the extreme super
heated plasma during re-entry.

00:09:31.510 --> 00:09:34.490
There were no windows in the middeck, below
the crew deck.

00:09:34.490 --> 00:09:39.120
The middeck is where many of the science experiments
in orbit were performed, and this is where

00:09:39.120 --> 00:09:40.900
the astronauts slept and ate.

00:09:40.900 --> 00:09:45.240
There were stations where the astronauts could
strap themselves to the walls in the weightless

00:09:45.240 --> 00:09:48.320
environment, but not everyone chose to use
them.

00:09:48.320 --> 00:09:52.019
The commander and pilot generally slept up
on the flight deck that they would just loosely

00:09:52.019 --> 00:09:57.500
strap themselves in their commander in pilot
seats because that was a good way, and you

00:09:57.500 --> 00:09:58.920
want to be kind of strapped in.

00:09:58.920 --> 00:10:03.839
There were some astronauts like story Musgrave
that liked to free float around in space,

00:10:03.839 --> 00:10:09.390
but he would bump into people, and I heard
that it was quite annoying sometimes.

00:10:09.390 --> 00:10:14.720
But the rest of us, you'd have a cloth sleeping
bag that's made out of just light material

00:10:14.720 --> 00:10:19.589
like this where you velcro it to a bulkhead
or the ceiling and you just zip yourself up

00:10:19.589 --> 00:10:20.600
in it.

00:10:20.600 --> 00:10:23.880
The time has now come to open the payload
doors.

00:10:23.880 --> 00:10:29.269
The doors needed to open shortly after reaching
orbit as one of the space shuttles vital life

00:10:29.269 --> 00:10:32.209
support systems required them to be open.

00:10:32.209 --> 00:10:34.329
The temperature control system.

00:10:34.329 --> 00:10:39.200
Two radiator panels are attached to each of
the forward payload bay doors.

00:10:39.200 --> 00:10:41.820
Freon filled tubes snake around these panels.

00:10:41.820 --> 00:10:46.940
Where they radiate heat absorbed from a heat
exchanger located inside the orbiter into

00:10:46.940 --> 00:10:47.940
space.

00:10:47.940 --> 00:10:52.300
The payload doors themselves are made from
a graphite/epoxy composite material which

00:10:52.300 --> 00:10:58.889
reduced the weight of the doors by 408 kilograms
over a comparable aluminium design.

00:10:58.889 --> 00:11:04.070
At the time this was the largest aerospace
composite structure ever made.

00:11:04.070 --> 00:11:05.070
[Page 173]

00:11:05.070 --> 00:11:10.230
Covering the payload bay which was 18.3 meters
long and 4.6 meters wide.

00:11:10.230 --> 00:11:17.279
The largest and heaviest payload it ever carried
was on STS-93 with the Chandra X-Ray Observatory

00:11:17.279 --> 00:11:20.660
coming in at 22.7 tonnes.

00:11:20.660 --> 00:11:22.870
Maxing out the shuttle's capacity.

00:11:22.870 --> 00:11:26.140
This mission came worryingly close to disaster.

00:11:26.140 --> 00:11:31.040
During each refurbishment of the shuttles
main engines, they were inspected with ultrasound

00:11:31.040 --> 00:11:33.190
[REF] to find signs of damage.

00:11:33.190 --> 00:11:38.100
In some cases damage may be detected in one
of the liquid oxygen posts that delivered

00:11:38.100 --> 00:11:43.760
liquid oxygen to the combustion chamber, and
instead of replacing the entire expensive

00:11:43.760 --> 00:11:49.149
and difficult to manufacture injection assembly,
the post was simply deactivated with a gold

00:11:49.149 --> 00:11:51.029
pin that plugged the channel.

00:11:51.029 --> 00:11:55.980
During the pre-launch start up sequence of
the main shuttle engines on STS-93

00:11:55.980 --> 00:12:00.880
one of these gold pins was ejected out and
struck the hydrogen coolant channels in the

00:12:00.880 --> 00:12:05.990
nozzle, resulting in a hydrogen leak that
you can actually see here in images of the

00:12:05.990 --> 00:12:06.990
launch.

00:12:06.990 --> 00:12:11.720
The engine detected a decrease in combustion
chamber pressure, and opened the oxidiser

00:12:11.720 --> 00:12:17.370
valve to increase thrust, the engine had no
way of knowing the issue was a hydrogen leak.

00:12:17.370 --> 00:12:22.130
It could only detect the drop in combustion
chamber pressure and respond by making the

00:12:22.130 --> 00:12:26.990
combustion less fuel rich, which increases
thrust and temperature.

00:12:26.990 --> 00:12:32.260
This incident resulted in a maintenance policy
switch that meant damaged oxygen posts had

00:12:32.260 --> 00:12:34.639
to be replaced and not simply plugged.

00:12:34.639 --> 00:12:39.430
Thankfully the engine survived and the mission
was successfully completed despite the main

00:12:39.430 --> 00:12:41.550
engines shutting down prematurely

00:12:41.550 --> 00:12:45.640
as a result of the oxygen depletion sensors
being triggered.

00:12:45.640 --> 00:12:51.190
So the orbiter was 4.6 m/s off it’s target
orbital velocity.

00:12:51.190 --> 00:12:56.620
Thankfully the Orbiter has an orbital maneuvering
system that could bring it up to its desired

00:12:56.620 --> 00:12:57.690
velocity

00:12:57.690 --> 00:13:02.480
These large rocket nozzles were not part of
the reaction control system, and were capable

00:13:02.480 --> 00:13:07.519
of providing 305 meters per second of additional
velocity.

00:13:07.519 --> 00:13:13.519
Powered by the same hypergolic fuel and oxidiser
as the RCS system, from tanks hidden within

00:13:13.519 --> 00:13:20.390
the OMS pods, capable of holding 4 tonnes
of fuel and 6.7 tonnes of oxidiser.

00:13:20.390 --> 00:13:25.100
When performing large orbital changes like
this it was important for the Space Shuttle

00:13:25.100 --> 00:13:27.180
to know precisely where it was,

00:13:27.180 --> 00:13:33.089
and to do this it could open these doors on
the left side of the orbiter's nose, revealing

00:13:33.089 --> 00:13:34.550
the star tracker optics.

00:13:34.550 --> 00:13:40.300
These star trackers analyzed positions of
the stars in the sky to determine exactly

00:13:40.300 --> 00:13:45.200
where the orbiter was, allowing it to precisely
navigate in orbit.

00:13:45.200 --> 00:13:50.350
Once the space shuttle has arrived at its
target orbit, the real work can begin.

00:13:50.350 --> 00:13:56.889
On Bruce’s mission, STS 49, the first task
was to rendezvous with that stranded communications

00:13:56.889 --> 00:13:57.889
satellite.

00:13:57.889 --> 00:14:01.850
Carefully maneuvering the orbiter closer to
the satellite as the satellite's operators

00:14:01.850 --> 00:14:04.430
attempted to stabilize its spin.

00:14:04.430 --> 00:14:06.510
As they approached 2 astronauts.

00:14:06.510 --> 00:14:11.660
Pierre Thout and Richard Hieb donned their
EVA suits in the middeck and prepared to enter

00:14:11.660 --> 00:14:13.050
the airlock.

00:14:13.050 --> 00:14:18.500
The airlock for this mission was located inside
the crew cabin of the middeck,but it could

00:14:18.500 --> 00:14:22.070
be configured to be outside in the payload
area.

00:14:22.070 --> 00:14:26.680
During the construction of the international
space station, a special docking system was

00:14:26.680 --> 00:14:31.529
also installed, which was required to allow
the space shuttle to dock to both Russian

00:14:31.529 --> 00:14:33.580
and American spacecraft.

00:14:33.580 --> 00:14:37.250
It was called the androgynous peripheral attach
system.

00:14:37.250 --> 00:14:41.610
The androgynous name comes from the fact that
both the target and the receiving end look

00:14:41.610 --> 00:14:46.880
identical, with no male or female parts, with
the goal of this being a universal docking

00:14:46.880 --> 00:14:50.380
system allowing any two spacecraft to dock
together.

00:14:50.380 --> 00:14:52.180
As Goddard intended.

00:14:52.180 --> 00:14:55.730
Each side had a ring with three petals equally
distributed.

00:14:55.730 --> 00:15:01.029
One side extended its mating ring for a soft
capture, where the 3 petals of each side come

00:15:01.029 --> 00:15:03.290
together and connect with these latches.

00:15:03.290 --> 00:15:04.290
[REF]

00:15:04.290 --> 00:15:09.470
At this point, the soft capture ring is retracted
and 12 latches engage to form an airtight

00:15:09.470 --> 00:15:12.320
connection between the two spacecraft.

00:15:12.320 --> 00:15:17.199
This system was used extensively during the
construction of the international space station,

00:15:17.199 --> 00:15:22.290
which the space shuttle had an instrumental
role in transporting and assembling in space.

00:15:22.290 --> 00:15:28.800
Including the very first modules to be connected
in 1998 on STS 88, when the orbiter docked

00:15:28.800 --> 00:15:34.870
with the Russian Zarya module using this docking
ring, and proceeded to connect the unity module.

00:15:34.870 --> 00:15:39.699
On STS-49, Bruce’s mission, the stranded
satellite was never intended to be docked

00:15:39.699 --> 00:15:45.220
with and had no docking points, and thus to
capture the stranded satellite the astronauts

00:15:45.220 --> 00:15:50.910
needed to perform an EVA and manually grab
the satellite using a capture bar specifically

00:15:50.910 --> 00:15:54.930
designed for this mission, but not everything
went to plan

00:15:54.930 --> 00:16:00.170
So we made several attempts to grab this satellite
with the capture bar, but every time Pierre

00:16:00.170 --> 00:16:05.920
touched the bottom of the satellite with the
capture bar, it bounced off out of control.

00:16:05.920 --> 00:16:10.990
Fortunately, the engineers that owned Intel
sat, they could send commands back up to it,

00:16:10.990 --> 00:16:15.450
restabilize it so we could maneuver back closer
to it and try again.

00:16:15.450 --> 00:16:20.620
So we tried three or four times the first
day to grab it every time it bounced off.

00:16:20.620 --> 00:16:23.880
Second day we tried again every time it bounced
off.

00:16:23.880 --> 00:16:26.560
So at that point we were kind of at a loss.

00:16:26.560 --> 00:16:28.680
We didn't know what we were going to do.

00:16:28.680 --> 00:16:31.590
And of course, all this time, I'm operating
the arm.

00:16:31.590 --> 00:16:34.750
I'm chasing the satellite with Pierre on the
end of it.

00:16:34.750 --> 00:16:38.360
And so it was a very demanding part of the
flight.

00:16:38.360 --> 00:16:43.730
So two days of trying the ground said, okay,
we're going to take the third day off, and

00:16:43.730 --> 00:16:47.910
then we're go back out and try on the fourth
day to do it again.

00:16:47.910 --> 00:16:50.750
Well, of course we felt like the mission was
lost.

00:16:50.750 --> 00:16:56.519
So the ground told us, go ahead, get a good
night's sleep, we'll call you in the morning.

00:16:56.519 --> 00:17:01.079
So we got to thinking, and matter of fact,
I saw that I could hear that Dan and Chile

00:17:01.079 --> 00:17:04.429
were talking up on the flight deck when we
were supposed to be asleep.

00:17:04.429 --> 00:17:09.380
So I floated up to the flight deck with them
and they were talking about how Endeavor was

00:17:09.380 --> 00:17:15.490
so maneuverable and was so efficient on its
fuel burn that they thought we could get close

00:17:15.490 --> 00:17:20.059
enough to the satellite to where Rick and
Pierre could just grab it with their gloved

00:17:20.059 --> 00:17:21.059
hands.

00:17:21.059 --> 00:17:26.539
And we saw it at one point when Pierre got
the capture bar, one side of the capture bar

00:17:26.539 --> 00:17:30.150
fired, and it almost immediately stopped the
rotation.

00:17:30.150 --> 00:17:34.030
So we knew that it was going to be easy to
stop the rotation if Rick and Pierre could

00:17:34.030 --> 00:17:36.890
just grab the bottom of the satellite.

00:17:36.890 --> 00:17:40.420
The problem was is now what do you do if you
do grab the satellite?

00:17:40.420 --> 00:17:45.870
You've got two guys out there holding a 9,000
pound satellite with no way to get it back

00:17:45.870 --> 00:17:51.320
on the rocket motor that we took up to strap
to it to send it back up to the proper orbit.

00:17:51.320 --> 00:17:57.030
Well, I scratched my head while I was up there
and I knew that because after we finished

00:17:57.030 --> 00:18:02.950
this satellite business, two other astronauts,
Tom Akers and Kathy Thornton, they were going

00:18:02.950 --> 00:18:06.620
to go out and do some practice of assembly
of the Space station.

00:18:06.620 --> 00:18:10.020
We hadn't built the space station at that
point, and they were going to be out trying

00:18:10.020 --> 00:18:11.539
to do some methods for that.

00:18:11.539 --> 00:18:18.280
Well, in order to accommodate all the different
sized astronauts, the EVA suits or the spacewalking

00:18:18.280 --> 00:18:23.700
suits, they're made out of different components,
different length arms, different size torsos,

00:18:23.700 --> 00:18:24.919
those sorts of things.

00:18:24.919 --> 00:18:32.190
And Rick Hebe is this big, Kathy Thornton
was this tall, and then Tom and Pierre are

00:18:32.190 --> 00:18:33.700
about my size.

00:18:33.700 --> 00:18:39.059
So in order to accommodate four astronauts
on two different spacewalks of those different

00:18:39.059 --> 00:18:44.690
sizes, we for the first time ever had three
full EVA suit, the components of three full

00:18:44.690 --> 00:18:46.790
EVA suits on board.

00:18:46.790 --> 00:18:49.160
So I got to thinking, I said, well, we have
three suits on board.

00:18:49.160 --> 00:18:51.830
Why don't we send three space walkers out?

00:18:51.830 --> 00:18:56.180
We can have two people grab it and then one
person put the capture bar on because the

00:18:56.180 --> 00:19:00.970
capture bar had to be attached to the satellite
because that was what was going to attach

00:19:00.970 --> 00:19:03.730
it to the rocket motor that we brought up.

00:19:03.730 --> 00:19:06.730
However, this plan had one problem.

00:19:06.730 --> 00:19:09.790
The airlock was only designed for two people.

00:19:09.790 --> 00:19:13.130
The airlock consisted of two air tight doors.

00:19:13.130 --> 00:19:18.030
Once inside, with the doors sealed, the astronauts
could depressurize the inside by venting the

00:19:18.030 --> 00:19:19.549
air to space.

00:19:19.549 --> 00:19:24.950
This could only be done from inside the hatch,
but repressurization could be done from outside

00:19:24.950 --> 00:19:25.970
and inside.

00:19:25.970 --> 00:19:28.020
For obvious safety reasons.

00:19:28.020 --> 00:19:32.530
This process took about 30 minutes and while
it happened the astronauts were connected

00:19:32.530 --> 00:19:37.450
to life support umbilicals so they didn't
have to waste their internal supplies during

00:19:37.450 --> 00:19:38.570
the wait.

00:19:38.570 --> 00:19:39.940
This was the first problem.

00:19:39.940 --> 00:19:43.539
There were only 2 life support umbilicals
available.

00:19:43.539 --> 00:19:49.460
It was also going to be a tight squeeze for
three astronauts with full EVA gear on, as

00:19:49.460 --> 00:19:57.510
the cylindrical airlock inside the mid-deck
was just 160 centimeters in diameter and 210

00:19:57.510 --> 00:19:58.820
centimeters high.

00:19:58.820 --> 00:20:02.990
The third astronaut was going to need to make
some sacrifices.

00:20:02.990 --> 00:20:07.059
So unlike normally where you just have two
astronauts, you put 'em in the airlock, you

00:20:07.059 --> 00:20:09.000
close the door with Tom,

00:20:09.000 --> 00:20:15.770
he had to go on all of his internal controls
and we float him in the airlock upside down.

00:20:15.770 --> 00:20:21.440
So now you've got Rick and Pierre right side
up, and Tom Akers head down in the airlock,

00:20:21.440 --> 00:20:25.080
and he's on his internal systems now where
Rick and Pierre are still attached to the

00:20:25.080 --> 00:20:30.880
umbilicals inside, close the airlock evacuate
it, and then all three of them floated out.

00:20:30.880 --> 00:20:33.580
And here, the three astronauts got to work.

00:20:33.580 --> 00:20:38.770
With Bruce on the remote manipulator arm controls
inside the orbiter, and he was chosen for

00:20:38.770 --> 00:20:40.549
a very specific reason.

00:20:40.549 --> 00:20:42.940
He was a coast guard helicopter pilot.

00:20:42.940 --> 00:20:47.300
And the reason Dan wanted to have a Coast
Guard helicopter pilot on the arm, and the

00:20:47.300 --> 00:20:54.030
reason for that is, is that operating the
arm, the controls that you use are vaguely

00:20:54.030 --> 00:20:58.690
similar to operating a helicopter because
in a helicopter you have to fly with both

00:20:58.690 --> 00:20:59.980
hands and both feet.

00:20:59.980 --> 00:21:03.860
Well, when you're operating the arm, you've
got two controllers, you've got a rotational

00:21:03.860 --> 00:21:07.540
hand controller and a translational hand controller.

00:21:07.540 --> 00:21:12.950
So the translational hand controller, you
move up, down, sideways, in and out.

00:21:12.950 --> 00:21:16.470
Rotational hand controller controls the attitude
of the arm.

00:21:16.470 --> 00:21:21.580
So it's very much flying a helicopter and
you can't be thinking about it when you're

00:21:21.580 --> 00:21:24.290
in a very tight situation.

00:21:24.290 --> 00:21:26.360
You've got to be the arm.

00:21:26.360 --> 00:21:32.669
The canadarm, named after its country of origin,
was a multifunctional tool used for many tasks

00:21:32.669 --> 00:21:33.929
aboard the shuttle.

00:21:33.929 --> 00:21:40.010
A simple articulated arm may not seem overly
impressive, but the unique physics of a weightless

00:21:40.010 --> 00:21:45.529
environment and desire to minimize the launch
weight of the space shuttle made for a very

00:21:45.529 --> 00:21:47.980
interesting engineering challenge.

00:21:47.980 --> 00:21:54.770
The entire 15 meter long arm weighs just 430
kilograms, and despite being able to move

00:21:54.770 --> 00:21:59.550
30 tonnes in orbit, it couldn’t even hold
up its own weight on earth.

00:21:59.550 --> 00:22:00.550
[REF]

00:22:00.550 --> 00:22:04.520
The booms were made from low weight epoxy
graphite composite, with 6 joints.

00:22:04.520 --> 00:22:09.909
2 joints at its shoulder for yaw and pitch,
1 elbow pitch joint, and 3 joints at the wrist

00:22:09.909 --> 00:22:11.740
for yaw, pitch and roll.

00:22:11.740 --> 00:22:16.549
For a total of 6 degrees of freedom of movement,
exactly like your own arm.

00:22:16.549 --> 00:22:22.600
However your elbow joint is only capable of
about 140 degrees of pitch before your biceps

00:22:22.600 --> 00:22:23.779
get in the way.

00:22:23.779 --> 00:22:31.510
The Canadarms elbow joint rotational axis
is offset by 0.15 meters to allow up to 160

00:22:31.510 --> 00:22:32.750
degrees of rotation.

00:22:32.750 --> 00:22:33.750
[REF]

00:22:33.750 --> 00:22:37.440
Because the arm is working in a weightless
environment motors don’t need to be able

00:22:37.440 --> 00:22:39.720
to apply that much torque.

00:22:39.720 --> 00:22:44.190
Each motor could produce 0.7 newton meters
of torque.

00:22:44.190 --> 00:22:50.090
With no friction in space a small force can
move any mass, it just depends how long you

00:22:50.090 --> 00:22:51.490
want it to take.

00:22:51.490 --> 00:22:57.059
In the same way an ion thruster with thrusts
of just 25 millinewtons could theoretically

00:22:57.059 --> 00:23:00.950
get a spacecraft up to lightspeed if it had
enough propellant.

00:23:00.950 --> 00:23:04.360
However, the dangers of inertia are still
real.

00:23:04.360 --> 00:23:09.530
If a satellite was moving quickly relative
to the arm it could easily bend and break

00:23:09.530 --> 00:23:10.530
the arm.

00:23:10.530 --> 00:23:15.159
So slow delicate movement is the name of the
game and the motor can work in reverse to

00:23:15.159 --> 00:23:17.690
assist each joint's brake system.

00:23:17.690 --> 00:23:22.480
The loaded arm speed had a maximum speed of
just 6 centimeters per second.

00:23:22.480 --> 00:23:28.179
The arm had a unique end fixture designed
to grapple onto attachment points on satellites.

00:23:28.179 --> 00:23:33.559
However, because the intelsat was never intended
to be rendezvoused with, it didn't have any

00:23:33.559 --> 00:23:35.390
grapple points installed.

00:23:35.390 --> 00:23:40.770
So the capture bar had an attachment point
for the second stage rocket and had a grapple

00:23:40.770 --> 00:23:45.740
point allowing the canadarm to grab hold of
it once it was securely attached.

00:23:45.740 --> 00:23:49.960
The grapple point is a simple metallic pin
with three lobes at its base.

00:23:49.960 --> 00:23:50.960
[REF]

00:23:50.960 --> 00:23:54.210
This is all the arm needed to firmly grab
onto the intelsat.

00:23:54.210 --> 00:24:00.120
The end effector of the canadarm used a naturally
self centering wire grapple system to hold

00:24:00.120 --> 00:24:01.620
onto this pin.

00:24:01.620 --> 00:24:04.590
Three wires, each anchored at two points.

00:24:04.590 --> 00:24:09.440
One point is stationary, while the secondary
anchor point is attached to a rotating ring.

00:24:09.440 --> 00:24:10.440
[REF]

00:24:10.440 --> 00:24:14.970
Once Bruce had maneuvered the arm over the
grapple rod using the camera feed this rotating

00:24:14.970 --> 00:24:19.169
ring would turn and ensnare the pin, pulling
it to the center.

00:24:19.169 --> 00:24:24.659
The pin is then pulled inwards until the three
lobes at its base lock into corresponding

00:24:24.659 --> 00:24:27.260
cut outs on the outside grabble ring.

00:24:27.260 --> 00:24:30.159
A simple, but effective mechanism.

00:24:30.159 --> 00:24:34.670
With the satellite now secure the three astronauts
could get to work attaching the new second

00:24:34.670 --> 00:24:41.230
stage to the capture bar, which they did successfully,
allowing intelsat to raise itself to geostationary

00:24:41.230 --> 00:24:44.470
orbit where it operated for 21 years.

00:24:44.470 --> 00:24:47.610
STS 49 had another mission to complete.

00:24:47.610 --> 00:24:52.890
A test run of space shuttle building techniques,
but with their time in orbit running out.

00:24:52.890 --> 00:24:54.770
That mission lost a day.

00:24:54.770 --> 00:24:59.050
Life support systems onboard were supplied
with enough gasses for the planned duration

00:24:59.050 --> 00:25:00.660
of the mission.

00:25:00.660 --> 00:25:05.000
Spherical tanks in the mid fuselage contained
high pressure oxygen and nitrogen.

00:25:05.000 --> 00:25:07.680
With 3 nitrogen tanks and 1 oxygen.

00:25:07.680 --> 00:25:13.240
Used to create a breathable atmosphere of
80% nitrogen and 20% oxygen.

00:25:13.240 --> 00:25:17.520
Of course carbon dioxide needed to be scrubbed
from the air, and this was

00:25:17.520 --> 00:25:22.690
done by passing the cabin air through lithium
hydroxide canisters located underneath the

00:25:22.690 --> 00:25:23.890
middeck.

00:25:23.890 --> 00:25:28.690
These solid canisters would react with the
CO2 to remove it from the atmosphere and create

00:25:28.690 --> 00:25:30.550
water in the process.

00:25:30.550 --> 00:25:35.480
This was not a renewable solution so every
12 hours the crew would access and replace

00:25:35.480 --> 00:25:38.970
these canisters through this hatch on the
middeck bay.

00:25:38.970 --> 00:25:43.539
There were also oxygen and hydrogen tanks
used for the space shuttles power system.

00:25:43.539 --> 00:25:48.660
A fuel cell that reacted the hydrogen and
oxygen to produce water and electricity.

00:25:48.660 --> 00:25:52.260
These powered everything on board, and produced
water in the process.

00:25:52.260 --> 00:25:57.940
But this water carried some extra hydrogen
gas, so before the water could be used it

00:25:57.940 --> 00:26:02.740
had to go through a process of degassing,
which separates the hydrogen and dumps it

00:26:02.740 --> 00:26:03.740
overboard.

00:26:03.740 --> 00:26:08.210
It was now time to prepare for re-entry and
the first step was closing those payload doors,

00:26:08.210 --> 00:26:11.940
but on STS 49 another problem was encountered.

00:26:11.940 --> 00:26:17.590
The payload doors would not latch due to thermal
warping of the doors, requiring some creative

00:26:17.590 --> 00:26:18.600
problem solving.

00:26:18.600 --> 00:26:23.320
Well, there's always two astronauts trained
to do EVAs and I was trained to do an EVA

00:26:23.320 --> 00:26:26.279
on my first flight, and that's for a contingency.

00:26:26.279 --> 00:26:31.280
If for example, the payload bay doors wouldn't
close or latch all the way when you're on

00:26:31.280 --> 00:26:36.370
orbit, we have special tools where we could
winch 'em closed and latch 'em all together.

00:26:36.370 --> 00:26:43.630
And matter of fact, on STS 49 after we had
trouble getting the satellite to launch because

00:26:43.630 --> 00:26:47.620
of a different switch configuration, we got
up there and the payload bay doors wouldn't

00:26:47.620 --> 00:26:48.870
close all the way.

00:26:48.870 --> 00:26:52.210
And so we thought, oh man, do we need to go
back out and do that?

00:26:52.210 --> 00:26:58.270
So your first attempt to fix something like
that is the thermal loads on the space shuttle.

00:26:58.270 --> 00:27:04.010
As you can imagine, you're 200 degrees on
the sunny side and 200 degrees below zero

00:27:04.010 --> 00:27:06.049
on the dark side.

00:27:06.049 --> 00:27:10.300
So a lot of times you'll get a little bit
of an oil canning effect or a little twisting

00:27:10.300 --> 00:27:14.429
of the fuselage just because of the thermal
effects.

00:27:14.429 --> 00:27:15.429
And that's what we did.

00:27:15.429 --> 00:27:19.720
We went ahead into what we call the barbecue
mode and we started, so we went ahead and

00:27:19.720 --> 00:27:24.230
orbited the earth and the barbecue mode to
try to equalize the temperatures on the shuttle

00:27:24.230 --> 00:27:27.700
when we couldn't get the payload bay doors
to close all the way.

00:27:27.700 --> 00:27:28.700
And

00:27:28.700 --> 00:27:30.710
When you say barbecue mode, do you mean like
a spit roast?

00:27:30.710 --> 00:27:31.730
It's rotating,

00:27:31.730 --> 00:27:32.730
Yes.

00:27:32.730 --> 00:27:36.330
Yeah, it's not that fast, but you do that
to where you try to equally heat while you're

00:27:36.330 --> 00:27:38.730
going through the sun and through the dark.

00:27:38.730 --> 00:27:42.440
And I think it was just one or, but we had
to do, and we tried to close the doors again

00:27:42.440 --> 00:27:44.029
and they latched.

00:27:44.029 --> 00:27:48.600
With the doors successfully closed, the space
shuttle can begin its preparations to re-enter

00:27:48.600 --> 00:27:50.000
the earth's atmosphere.

00:27:50.000 --> 00:27:55.690
Firing the OMS engines once more to slow the
orbital velocity and slowly bring it back

00:27:55.690 --> 00:27:57.490
into earth’s atmosphere.

00:27:57.490 --> 00:28:03.500
At the moment of reentry, the space shuttle
will be traveling between 7 and 8 km/s.

00:28:03.500 --> 00:28:07.399
Entering the thin upper atmosphere at 30 times
the speed of sound.

00:28:07.399 --> 00:28:13.159
A speed so great that it begins to rip air
molecules apart, creating a glowing cloud

00:28:13.159 --> 00:28:16.560
of charged plasma around the lower surface
of the orbiter.

00:28:16.560 --> 00:28:21.820
With peak temperatures reaching 1650 degree
celsius (3000 F).

00:28:21.820 --> 00:28:26.960
To not only withstand this immense heat, but
to transition to a glider capable of landing

00:28:26.960 --> 00:28:31.360
on a runway required some incredible innovative
engineering.

00:28:31.360 --> 00:28:35.570
Combining aerospace and aviation technologies
like never before.

00:28:35.570 --> 00:28:36.880
A true space plane.

00:28:36.880 --> 00:28:42.480
Episode 3 of the insane engineering of the
space shuttle, detailing the engineering of

00:28:42.480 --> 00:28:47.600
re-entry and landing will be uploaded in the
